```c
if (static_cpu_has_bug(X86_BUG_SYSRET_SS_ATTRS)) {
    unsigned short ss_sel;
    savesegment(ss, ss_sel);
    if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
        loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
}
```

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
    loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
```
Reason:
The issue arises because AMD CPUs do not reinitialize the SS descriptor on executing the SYSRET instruction. If SYSRET is executed with SS set to NULL, it results in an invalid user mode state where SS appears to be equal to __USER_DS but is actually unusable, leading to a #SS fault when accessed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
savesegment(ss, ss_sel);
if (ss_sel != __KERNEL_DS)
    loadsegment(ss, __KERNEL_DS);
```
Reason:
The fix involves checking the current value of the SS segment selector and ensuring it is set to __KERNEL_DS during every context switch. This prevents the scenario where SYSRET could be executed with SS set to NULL, thus avoiding the invalid user mode state and the subsequent #SS fault.

